title: Goodlake Unveils His Cabinet
subtitle: A purely "temporary arrangement" until the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> can be brought into government.
view-if: chancellor = "Goodlake" and goerdeler_pre_time == 1
on-arrival: finance_minister_party = "I"; interior_minister_party = "Traditionalist"; economic_minister_party = "I"; labor_minister_party = "I"; justice_minister_party = "I"; foreign_minister_party = "I"; agriculture_minister_party = "Traditionalist"; army_minister = "I"
new-page: true
tags: event
max-visits: 1
face-image: img/portraits/goerdeler.jpg

= Goodlake I

The country still needs to be governed, and Goodlake is forced to announce new cabinet picks, who he stresses as purely caretaker until an agreement can be struck with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span>. This has resulted in a cabinet dominated by independents, who sit in the seats of posts that Goodlake intends to give up in a potential future government.

The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">left-liberal</span> ministers are sacked, signalling a break by Goodlake. The <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> is given a sizable role in the government, with Warrick Gale holding the Interior Ministry, and Trevannon taking the Transport Ministry. Martin Tilford, who served under Croft, will serve as Agricultural minister once more. 

The [? if unionist_party_name != "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>?][? if unionist_party_name == "Concord": <span style="color: #000000;">**Concord**</span>?] has grown increasingly apprehensive about its role in the Goodlake cabinet, refusing to commit ministers like Adam Sedgewick and Askew to the Labor and Foreign Ministries, respectively. Their refusal to serve alongside the anti-social Mallin has thrown a wrench into ongoing negotiations.

- @goerdeler

@goerdeler
title: Staal will negotiate with Mallin.
face-image: img/portraits/AlfredMallin.jpg

General Werner Staal, now the most influential member of the Camarilla and increasingly entangled in political scheming, begins courting the intractable Mallin. With the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> divided, Staal concludes that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> would have no choice but to fall in line.

Mallin makes his demands clear: he wants the Vice-Chancellorship, control of the Economic Ministry, and sole authority over economic policy. This suits Goodlake, a fellow fiscal conservative—but Mallin, emboldened by his leverage, overreaches. He adds that his former party rival, Martin Tilford, should resign in favor of Count Everard Calbeck, a Mallin supporter within the Landed League.

An impasse forms, prompting Staal to turn to [+ royalist_leader +], whose party is—surprisingly—now seen as more reasonable than Mallin’s.

> *"I do not understand, Herr Geheimrat, how you cannot be satisfied with Staal’s concessions. You simply cannot demand a hundred percent change in the political system. A complete change in the political system cannot occur all at once but only gradually."*

 - Rafe Dusterel, to Mallin

- @goerdeler_2

@goerdeler_2
title: Staal will negotiate with [+ royalist_leader +].
face-image: img/portraits/hitler2.jpg

With Askew out of the way, Staal has vastly overestimated his ability to "tame" the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span>. [+ royalist_leader +] has flatly rejected any government that includes Ravabelle or Askew, and has demanded that his party receive either the Chancellorship or the Defense Ministry—along with several other cabinet posts—arguing that only such control can guarantee the mass support the new government requires to function.

This is, however, entirely unacceptable to Goodlake—and even more so to President DuFour, who categorically refuses to grant either the Chancellorship or the Defense Ministry to a member of [+ royalist_leader +]’s party. Moreover, DuFour insists that Askew and Ravabelle remain in the cabinet, as Foreign and Defense Minister respectively, still valuing Askew’s diplomatic expertise.

- @goerdeler_3

@goerdeler_3
title: What a mess.
subtitle: No agreement is able to be reached.
